    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Have you ever been distracted by technology? You are not alone. Reports on student surveys show that digital distractions are interfering with their homework, personal relationships, and sleep. Though the amount of digital content has increased exponentially during the last decade, human cognitive control has not advanced at the same rate. The growing pervasiveness of technology has exposed students to an increasing number of challenges, such as keeping on-task, avoiding distraction, monitoring attention and managing goals. This effect has grown exponentially due to the rise of advertisement as a dominant business model in the technology companies most present in students' lives (e.g. Google and Facebook). Consequently, individual study time is increasingly ineffective because students are vulnerable to negative external distractions, but most importantly because technology reinforces beliefs of lack of ability to develop self-control.  Our project explores the disempowering effect of technology on students’ sense of agency, and the vulnerability of students from underprivileged environments to overcome digital distractions. Through design-based research we created Inspireo, a mobile companion on Android to enhance learners’ beliefs in their capabilities to think and behave towards their goals, and their ability to self-regulate in an increasingly persuasive digital world. The pilot results on users after using inspireo show significant and large effects of improvement in self-efficacy on self-regulated learning, triangulated with self-reported qualitative data. Furthermore, we analyze equity gaps by socio-economic status, formulate a model for technologies that level the field of learning opportunities, and advocate for the development of technologies that *actually* empower students.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
